1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,410
Tokyo Bay, the second of September,

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:04,810
1945.

3
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:07,542
It is four minutes before nine in the

4
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:09,591
morning, and the sky is grey.

5
00:00:09,611 --> 00:00:11,981
A small boat comes alongside the American

6
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:13,689
battleship U S S Missouri.

7
00:00:13,709 --> 00:00:16,420
Eleven Japanese men climb out of it and

8
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:17,786
start up the ladder.

9
00:00:17,806 --> 00:00:19,835
The first of them climbs slowly,

10
00:00:19,855 --> 00:00:22,567
with a cane, because he walks on an

11
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:23,387
artificial leg.

12
00:00:24,525 --> 00:00:28,289
At the top of the ladder there is no room

13
00:00:28,309 --> 00:00:29,045
to stand.

14
00:00:29,065 --> 00:00:31,694
Sailors fill every turret top and every

15
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:32,451
gun platform.

16
00:00:32,471 --> 00:00:34,342
Photographers hang from the superstructure.

17
00:00:34,362 --> 00:00:36,613
Admiral Nimitz wrote that evening that

18
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:38,883
every point of vantage was taken,

19
00:00:38,903 --> 00:00:41,910
by newsmen, by cameramen, and by any man

20
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:44,936
from the ship who could get a foothold.

21
00:00:44,956 --> 00:00:47,605
More than two hundred correspondents are aboard.

22
00:00:48,975 --> 00:00:51,195
One of the eleven is a diplomat named

23
00:00:51,215 --> 00:00:53,655
Toshikazu Kase.

24
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:56,103
Years later he wrote down what it felt

25
00:00:56,123 --> 00:00:58,246
like to walk out onto that deck.

26
00:00:58,266 --> 00:01:00,694
He felt, he wrote, that they were being

27
00:01:00,714 --> 00:01:01,918
put in the pillory.

28
00:01:01,938 --> 00:01:04,672
A million eyes seemed to beat on them like

29
00:01:04,692 --> 00:01:05,897
a storm of arrows.

30
00:01:05,917 --> 00:01:08,039
Never, he wrote, had he realized that

31
00:01:08,059 --> 00:01:09,895
staring eyes could hurt so much.

32
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,919
They are led to a spot in front of a table

33
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:15,210
covered with a green cloth,

34
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:16,500
and left to stand there.

35
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,845
Kase wrote that they waited like penitent

36
00:01:19,865 --> 00:01:21,599
boys awaiting the dreaded schoolmaster.

37
00:01:21,619 --> 00:01:24,757
While he waited, he looked up at the wall

38
00:01:24,777 --> 00:01:25,809
of the ship.

39
00:01:25,829 --> 00:01:28,967
Painted on it was a row of little Rising

40
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:31,422
Sun flags, one for every Japanese plane

41
00:01:31,442 --> 00:01:33,177
and submarine the Missouri's gunners

42
00:01:33,197 --> 00:01:34,600
claimed to have destroyed.

43
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:38,692
He tried to count them, and tears gathered

44
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:39,728
in his eyes.

45
00:01:39,748 --> 00:01:42,145
He thought of the young suicide pilots.

46
00:01:42,165 --> 00:01:44,216
He was sure of one thing.

47
00:01:44,236 --> 00:01:46,978
He had been brought here to be humiliated,

48
00:01:46,998 --> 00:01:49,070
in front of the whole world.

49
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:53,004
He was wrong about what would happen in

50
00:01:53,024 --> 00:01:54,366
the next twenty minutes.

51
00:01:54,386 --> 00:01:56,409
And before that day was over,

52
00:01:56,429 --> 00:01:58,793
this quiet diplomat would put a question

53
00:01:58,813 --> 00:02:01,176
in writing to the Emperor of Japan.

54
00:02:01,196 --> 00:02:03,560
This is the documented story of the

55
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:05,943
eighteen days that ended the Second World

56
00:02:05,963 --> 00:02:08,667
War, told from the records of both sides:

57
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:10,029
the Navy's radio messages,

58
00:02:10,049 --> 00:02:11,391
MacArthur's own staff history,

59
00:02:11,411 --> 00:02:14,115
the memoirs of the men who were there,

60
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:16,499
and the recording of the ceremony itself.

61
00:02:16,519 --> 00:02:18,115
To understand Kase's question,

62
00:02:18,135 --> 00:02:20,720
we have to go back to the day the war stopped.

63
00:02:22,425 --> 00:02:23,665
On the fifteenth of August,

64
00:02:23,685 --> 00:02:27,636
1945, the people of Japan heard their

65
00:02:27,656 --> 00:02:29,411
Emperor speak on the radio.

66
00:02:29,431 --> 00:02:31,897
He told them the war was over.

67
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:34,028
Japan had accepted the Allied terms.

68
00:02:34,048 --> 00:02:36,514
For the first time in its recorded

69
00:02:36,534 --> 00:02:39,000
history, the country would be occupied by

70
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:40,085
a foreign army.

71
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:44,027
Nobody knew what that would mean.

72
00:02:44,047 --> 00:02:46,725
MacArthur's own staff later collected what

73
00:02:46,745 --> 00:02:49,422
the Japanese newspapers had printed in

74
00:02:49,442 --> 00:02:50,771
those first days.

75
00:02:50,791 --> 00:02:53,674
The newspaper Yomiuri reported that within

76
00:02:53,694 --> 00:02:55,880
three days of the broadcast,

77
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,527
the people of Tokyo had convinced

78
00:02:58,547 --> 00:03:00,292
themselves of five things.

79
00:03:00,312 --> 00:03:02,498
American soldiers would loot Japan.

80
00:03:02,518 --> 00:03:06,027
Americans would rob Japan of all the food.

81
00:03:06,047 --> 00:03:08,674
Women and girls would be violated.

82
00:03:08,694 --> 00:03:10,880
All men would be killed.

83
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:14,430
What was left of Tokyo would be devastated.

84
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,877
The government that had to manage this was

85
00:03:18,897 --> 00:03:19,651
brand new.

86
00:03:19,671 --> 00:03:22,260
The Emperor's uncle, Prince Higashikuni,

87
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:23,440
became prime minister.

88
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:26,980
His job was to hand the country to the

89
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,947
Americans without a civil war.

90
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:31,307
Three to four million Japanese soldiers

91
00:03:31,327 --> 00:03:32,900
were still under arms.

92
00:03:34,175 --> 00:03:36,872
His first test was a single trip.

93
00:03:36,892 --> 00:03:39,589
The Allies had ordered Japan to send

94
00:03:39,609 --> 00:03:41,530
envoys to MacArthur's headquarters in

95
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:43,859
Manila, to receive the instructions for

96
00:03:43,879 --> 00:03:44,679
the occupation.

97
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:48,530
On the nineteenth of August,

98
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:50,555
sixteen Japanese officers led by

99
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:53,267
Lieutenant General Torashiro Kawabe took

100
00:03:53,287 --> 00:03:54,414
off for Manila.

101
00:03:54,434 --> 00:03:57,473
The Allies had ordered how they must travel.

102
00:03:57,493 --> 00:03:59,767
They flew in two twin-engine Betty

103
00:03:59,787 --> 00:04:02,443
bombers, painted white all over with green

104
00:04:02,463 --> 00:04:03,263
crosses.

105
00:04:04,225 --> 00:04:06,605
Their call signs were Bataan One and

106
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:07,830
Bataan Two.

107
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:10,545
The bombers flew to the island of Ie

108
00:04:10,565 --> 00:04:12,601
Shima, where American cameramen filmed

109
00:04:12,621 --> 00:04:13,339
them landing.

110
00:04:13,359 --> 00:04:16,291
From there the envoys were put on an

111
00:04:16,311 --> 00:04:18,525
American transport and flown to Manila.

112
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,260
The Navy's historian later summed up the

113
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:24,300
talks in Manila in one sentence:

114
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:25,660
there were no negotiations.

115
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:29,780
Kawabe's men were handed the orders and

116
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,527
the dates.

117
00:04:30,547 --> 00:04:32,040
Then they started home.

118
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:35,361
They did not arrive on time.

119
00:04:35,381 --> 00:04:38,445
On the way back, one of the white planes

120
00:04:38,465 --> 00:04:41,186
had to be ditched, and the delegation was

121
00:04:41,206 --> 00:04:42,214
missing for hours.

122
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:44,806
In Tokyo, Prime Minister Higashikuni sat

123
00:04:44,826 --> 00:04:45,829
up all night.

124
00:04:45,849 --> 00:04:48,215
He later wrote that he was wondering

125
00:04:48,235 --> 00:04:50,601
whether Japan's own air attack units had

126
00:04:50,621 --> 00:04:51,985
shot the envoys down.

127
00:04:53,275 --> 00:04:55,666
When Kawabe finally reached the palace,

128
00:04:55,686 --> 00:04:59,644
the terms were not as severe as Tokyo had feared.

129
00:04:59,664 --> 00:05:00,837
MacArthur's staff history,

130
00:05:00,857 --> 00:05:02,750
quoting Higashikuni's memoirs,

131
00:05:02,770 --> 00:05:05,480
says the Emperor was quite relieved.

132
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:09,016
But Higashikuni's fear that night was not

133
00:05:09,036 --> 00:05:09,428
imagination.

134
00:05:09,448 --> 00:05:11,901
At the very airfield where MacArthur

135
00:05:11,921 --> 00:05:14,786
intended to land, a Japanese navy captain

136
00:05:14,806 --> 00:05:16,455
had refused to surrender.

137
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:20,690
The airfield was called Atsugi,

138
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:22,890
about twenty-five miles southwest of Tokyo.

139
00:05:22,910 --> 00:05:27,406
It was the home of the 302nd Naval Air Group.

140
00:05:27,426 --> 00:05:30,394
It had fought right up to the morning of

141
00:05:30,414 --> 00:05:32,718
the fifteenth of August, the last morning

142
00:05:32,738 --> 00:05:33,714
of the war.

143
00:05:33,734 --> 00:05:36,038
It had only ever had one commander,

144
00:05:36,058 --> 00:05:37,610
Captain Yasuna Kozono.

145
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,433
After the Emperor's broadcast,

146
00:05:40,453 --> 00:05:42,250
Kozono called his officers together.

147
00:05:42,270 --> 00:05:44,793
According to a study published by Japan's

148
00:05:44,813 --> 00:05:47,700
own Ministry of Defense, he told them the

149
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:50,243
Emperor had been deceived by his advisers,

150
00:05:50,263 --> 00:05:53,150
and that Japan did not know how to

151
00:05:53,170 --> 00:05:54,967
surrender and could not surrender.

152
00:05:54,987 --> 00:05:58,237
Then he sent a radio message to the entire

153
00:05:58,257 --> 00:05:58,963
Japanese navy.

154
00:05:58,983 --> 00:06:01,870
It said that its leaders had lost the

155
00:06:01,890 --> 00:06:02,980
right to command.

156
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:06,289
He had about a thousand men,

157
00:06:06,309 --> 00:06:08,248
around a hundred and thirty aircraft,

158
00:06:08,268 --> 00:06:10,534
many of them damaged, and supplies for

159
00:06:10,554 --> 00:06:11,187
three months.

160
00:06:11,207 --> 00:06:13,473
From the sixteenth to the eighteenth of

161
00:06:13,493 --> 00:06:15,758
August, his planes flew over bases and

162
00:06:15,778 --> 00:06:18,044
cities and dropped leaflets calling on the

163
00:06:18,064 --> 00:06:19,370
country to fight on.

164
00:06:20,625 --> 00:06:23,128
A rear admiral was sent to talk him out of

165
00:06:23,148 --> 00:06:23,885
it, and failed.

166
00:06:23,905 --> 00:06:27,925
Kase later wrote that men at the air base

167
00:06:27,945 --> 00:06:30,182
were even boasting that they would attack

168
00:06:30,202 --> 00:06:31,150
the battleship Missouri.

169
00:06:31,170 --> 00:06:33,408
MacArthur's plane was supposed to land on

170
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:35,685
this airfield in a matter of days.

171
00:06:37,025 --> 00:06:39,891
The navy did not dare to send troops

172
00:06:39,911 --> 00:06:41,334
against its own men.

173
00:06:41,354 --> 00:06:44,219
What stopped Captain Kozono in the end was

174
00:06:44,239 --> 00:06:45,301
not an order.

175
00:06:45,321 --> 00:06:47,125
It was something much smaller.

176
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:49,926
Kozono had malaria.

177
00:06:49,946 --> 00:06:53,215
It had come back on the day of the

178
00:06:53,235 --> 00:06:55,408
surrender broadcast, and as the rebellion

179
00:06:55,428 --> 00:06:57,235
went on, it got worse.

180
00:06:57,255 --> 00:06:59,794
By the eighteenth of August he was

181
00:06:59,814 --> 00:07:01,255
confined to his quarters.

182
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:04,179
The navy's doctors had to sedate him again

183
00:07:04,199 --> 00:07:04,999
and again.

184
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:08,070
On the evening of the twentieth,

185
00:07:08,090 --> 00:07:09,603
two of his senior officers,

186
00:07:09,623 --> 00:07:12,230
Commander Hideo Sugawara and Lieutenant

187
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:14,950
Commander Minoru Yoshino, were received by

188
00:07:14,970 --> 00:07:17,631
Prince Takamatsu, the Emperor's younger

189
00:07:17,651 --> 00:07:19,834
brother and himself a navy officer.

190
00:07:19,854 --> 00:07:22,771
The prince told them the surrender was the

191
00:07:22,791 --> 00:07:23,873
Emperor's own wish.

192
00:07:23,893 --> 00:07:26,830
It was not a plot by his advisers.

193
00:07:28,225 --> 00:07:30,358
Early the next morning, the twenty-first,

194
00:07:30,378 --> 00:07:32,870
Kozono was heavily sedated and moved to

195
00:07:32,890 --> 00:07:35,965
the naval hospital at Yokosuka.

196
00:07:35,985 --> 00:07:38,920
Without him, the revolt broke apart in a

197
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:39,658
single day.

198
00:07:39,678 --> 00:07:42,244
About eighty young pilots and aircrew took

199
00:07:42,264 --> 00:07:44,829
thirty-four planes and flew them to Army

200
00:07:44,849 --> 00:07:47,414
airfields, hoping to keep fighting from there.

201
00:07:47,434 --> 00:07:49,630
They were brought back and arrested.

202
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:52,216
The propellers were taken off every plane

203
00:07:52,236 --> 00:07:55,698
at Atsugi and the fuel tanks were drained.

204
00:07:55,718 --> 00:07:59,645
That afternoon the 302nd Air Group was disbanded.

205
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,362
A week later, the first Americans to land

206
00:08:03,382 --> 00:08:06,437
at Atsugi found rows of Japanese planes

207
00:08:06,457 --> 00:08:09,312
sitting helplessly, stripped of their propellers.

208
00:08:09,332 --> 00:08:12,188
Kozono was court-martialed in October for

209
00:08:12,208 --> 00:08:14,585
group insubordination and sentenced to

210
00:08:14,605 --> 00:08:16,023
life in prison.

211
00:08:16,043 --> 00:08:20,946
He was paroled in 1950, and died ten years later.

212
00:08:20,966 --> 00:08:23,420
But while Atsugi was being emptied,

213
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,443
the sound of B-29s came back over Japan,

214
00:08:26,463 --> 00:08:29,080
and people ran for the shelters out of habit.

215
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:32,587
From the twenty-seventh of August,

216
00:08:32,607 --> 00:08:34,033
the Superfortresses came back.

217
00:08:34,053 --> 00:08:36,201
They flew low over the cities,

218
00:08:36,221 --> 00:08:39,092
and people who had lived through the fire

219
00:08:39,112 --> 00:08:41,622
raids ran for the air raid shelters.

220
00:08:41,642 --> 00:08:44,152
MacArthur's staff wrote that it was force

221
00:08:44,172 --> 00:08:44,874
of habit.

222
00:08:44,894 --> 00:08:46,340
There were no bombs.

223
00:08:47,750 --> 00:08:50,243
The planes had words painted under each

224
00:08:50,263 --> 00:08:51,679
wing in big letters:

225
00:08:51,699 --> 00:08:52,756
P W Supplies.

226
00:08:52,776 --> 00:08:55,269
They were looking for the prison camps.

227
00:08:55,289 --> 00:08:57,782
Under every camp roof, Allied prisoners of

228
00:08:57,802 --> 00:09:00,295
war had been starving for three years,

229
00:09:00,315 --> 00:09:04,610
some since the fall of Bataan and Corregidor.

230
00:09:04,630 --> 00:09:06,731
Now the B-29s dropped them food,

231
00:09:06,751 --> 00:09:08,694
medicine and clothing, packed in bundles

232
00:09:08,714 --> 00:09:11,330
meant to last three, seven or ten days.

233
00:09:12,675 --> 00:09:15,565
The prisoners painted answers on their

234
00:09:15,585 --> 00:09:19,445
roofs, big enough to read from the air.

235
00:09:19,465 --> 00:09:21,870
The staff history lists them.

236
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:23,325
S O S.

237
00:09:23,345 --> 00:09:25,265
Have hospital cases here.

238
00:09:25,285 --> 00:09:26,720
Drop radio please.

239
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:29,145
Hong Kong men thank you.

240
00:09:29,165 --> 00:09:32,163
Men from Corregidor, Bataan thank Wasp.

241
00:09:32,183 --> 00:09:34,915
And on one roof, after the trucks had

242
00:09:34,935 --> 00:09:35,259
come:

243
00:09:35,279 --> 00:09:36,655
All left this camp.

244
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:39,718
Not every drop went well.

245
00:09:39,738 --> 00:09:41,924
Some drums broke loose from their

246
00:09:41,944 --> 00:09:44,497
parachutes and fell straight through camp roofs.

247
00:09:44,517 --> 00:09:46,703
Eight of the bombers were lost.

248
00:09:46,723 --> 00:09:49,277
Over the next three weeks the Twentieth

249
00:09:49,297 --> 00:09:51,483
Air Force flew nine hundred effective

250
00:09:51,503 --> 00:09:53,321
sorties, dropped four thousand four

251
00:09:53,341 --> 00:09:55,527
hundred and seventy tons of supplies,

252
00:09:55,547 --> 00:09:58,120
and reached a hundred and fifty-eight camps.

253
00:09:59,375 --> 00:10:02,004
But food could be dropped from the sky.

254
00:10:02,024 --> 00:10:04,985
Men who could not walk could not be lifted

255
00:10:05,005 --> 00:10:05,978
out by parachute.

256
00:10:05,998 --> 00:10:07,965
The Japanese government's own count said

257
00:10:07,985 --> 00:10:10,283
there were six thousand one hundred and

258
00:10:10,303 --> 00:10:12,270
twenty-five Allied prisoners in the Tokyo

259
00:10:12,290 --> 00:10:14,588
area, and that four hundred and seventeen

260
00:10:14,608 --> 00:10:17,237
of them could not get out of bed.

261
00:10:17,257 --> 00:10:19,575
To reach them, somebody had to land.

262
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:23,907
The first landing was set for the

263
00:10:23,927 --> 00:10:25,033
twenty-sixth of August.

264
00:10:25,053 --> 00:10:27,661
A typhoon pushed it back two days.

265
00:10:27,681 --> 00:10:30,288
In the meantime, an American Navy pilot

266
00:10:30,308 --> 00:10:32,920
from the carrier Yorktown landed at Atsugi

267
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:35,519
against orders, and had the Japanese hang

268
00:10:35,539 --> 00:10:37,375
a banner for the Army.

269
00:10:37,395 --> 00:10:38,118
It said:

270
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:40,320
Welcome to Atsugi from Third Fleet.

271
00:10:41,625 --> 00:10:43,649
At eight twenty-eight on the morning of

272
00:10:43,669 --> 00:10:45,595
the twenty-eighth, a C-46 transport

273
00:10:45,615 --> 00:10:48,305
touched down at Atsugi.

274
00:10:48,325 --> 00:10:50,682
It was followed by fifteen more.

275
00:10:50,702 --> 00:10:53,060
On board was the advance party:

276
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:56,229
about a hundred and fifty officers and men

277
00:10:56,249 --> 00:10:59,003
under Colonel Charles Tench of MacArthur's staff.

278
00:10:59,023 --> 00:11:01,776
The Japanese officer in charge of the

279
00:11:01,796 --> 00:11:04,325
airfield, Lieutenant General Seizo Arisue,

280
00:11:04,345 --> 00:11:06,530
later wrote in his memoir that the

281
00:11:06,550 --> 00:11:07,475
Americans landed downwind,

282
00:11:07,495 --> 00:11:10,645
ignoring the wind sock he had put up for them.

283
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:15,220
Arisue walked up to Colonel Tench and

284
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:16,465
stiffly saluted him.

285
00:11:16,485 --> 00:11:18,955
He offered the Americans a meal.

286
00:11:18,975 --> 00:11:20,200
Tench politely declined.

287
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:23,105
His men had brought their own rations.

288
00:11:23,125 --> 00:11:24,350
Within forty-five minutes,

289
00:11:24,370 --> 00:11:26,840
to the amazement of the Japanese,

290
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:31,010
the Signal Corps had radios on the air to Okinawa.

291
00:11:32,475 --> 00:11:34,594
Fewer than two hundred American soldiers

292
00:11:34,614 --> 00:11:37,089
with light weapons were standing in the

293
00:11:37,109 --> 00:11:39,940
middle of a country where three to four

294
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:42,079
million soldiers were still under arms.

295
00:11:42,099 --> 00:11:44,668
General Arisue wrote that he had never had

296
00:11:44,688 --> 00:11:47,024
to use his head and his body so hard for

297
00:11:47,044 --> 00:11:47,844
so long.

298
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:50,952
That same morning, the first of two

299
00:11:50,972 --> 00:11:52,503
hundred and fifty-eight Allied ships

300
00:11:52,523 --> 00:11:53,744
steamed into Tokyo Bay.

301
00:11:53,764 --> 00:11:55,916
And that night, out in the bay,

302
00:11:55,936 --> 00:11:58,398
an American picket boat picked up two men

303
00:11:58,418 --> 00:12:00,280
who should not have been there.

304
00:12:01,875 --> 00:12:04,194
The voices belonged to two British

305
00:12:04,214 --> 00:12:05,363
prisoners of war.

306
00:12:05,383 --> 00:12:07,702
They had escaped from a camp.

307
00:12:07,722 --> 00:12:09,651
Their story reached Admiral William

308
00:12:09,671 --> 00:12:11,989
Halsey, commander of the Third Fleet.

309
00:12:12,009 --> 00:12:15,498
The next day a Swiss doctor from the Red

310
00:12:15,518 --> 00:12:16,667
Cross confirmed it.

311
00:12:16,687 --> 00:12:19,006
In the camps around Tokyo Bay,

312
00:12:19,026 --> 00:12:20,195
prisoners were dying.

313
00:12:21,475 --> 00:12:23,723
The Red Cross gave the numbers.

314
00:12:23,743 --> 00:12:26,975
At a camp called Omori, on the waterfront,

315
00:12:26,995 --> 00:12:29,255
two hundred captured aviators were in

316
00:12:29,275 --> 00:12:30,395
extremely bad condition.

317
00:12:30,415 --> 00:12:33,055
At the hospital of a camp called

318
00:12:33,075 --> 00:12:35,778
Shinagawa, a hundred and fifty men were

319
00:12:35,798 --> 00:12:36,598
seriously ill.

320
00:12:37,875 --> 00:12:39,951
Halsey had three hospital ships ready,

321
00:12:39,971 --> 00:12:42,048
with doctors, corpsmen, food and clothing.

322
00:12:42,068 --> 00:12:44,494
But he had an order from MacArthur,

323
00:12:44,514 --> 00:12:46,590
sent on the twenty-fifth of August.

324
00:12:46,610 --> 00:12:49,386
It said it was not believed advisable for

325
00:12:49,406 --> 00:12:52,530
the Third Fleet to take any action on its

326
00:12:52,550 --> 00:12:55,695
own to evacuate prisoners in the Tokyo Bay area.

327
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,717
On the twenty-ninth, Fleet Admiral Chester

328
00:12:59,737 --> 00:13:01,997
Nimitz arrived in the bay.

329
00:13:02,017 --> 00:13:04,734
He sent MacArthur a radio message,

330
00:13:04,754 --> 00:13:07,927
urging him to let Halsey act immediately.

331
00:13:07,947 --> 00:13:11,576
Then he added a deadline of his own.

332
00:13:11,596 --> 00:13:15,225
If no reply came from MacArthur by one

333
00:13:15,245 --> 00:13:17,505
o'clock that afternoon, Halsey was

334
00:13:17,525 --> 00:13:19,786
authorized to do whatever humanitarian

335
00:13:19,806 --> 00:13:20,698
considerations required.

336
00:13:20,718 --> 00:13:23,891
In Halsey's memoir, Nimitz's words to him

337
00:13:23,911 --> 00:13:24,803
were shorter.

338
00:13:24,823 --> 00:13:25,716
Go ahead.

339
00:13:25,736 --> 00:13:27,560
General MacArthur will understand.

340
00:13:29,325 --> 00:13:31,794
That afternoon, landing boats went up the

341
00:13:31,814 --> 00:13:34,105
estuary toward Omori.

342
00:13:34,125 --> 00:13:36,648
The rescue was led by Commodore Rodger

343
00:13:36,668 --> 00:13:38,464
Simpson, with the hospital ship

344
00:13:38,484 --> 00:13:39,554
Benevolence behind him.

345
00:13:39,574 --> 00:13:41,733
Simpson's chief of staff was Commander

346
00:13:41,753 --> 00:13:44,276
Harold Stassen, who before the war had

347
00:13:44,296 --> 00:13:46,819
been the youngest governor in the history

348
00:13:46,839 --> 00:13:47,639
of Minnesota.

349
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,055
At the gate of the camp the Japanese

350
00:13:52,075 --> 00:13:53,208
commandant met them.

351
00:13:53,228 --> 00:13:55,515
Halsey's memoir records what he said:

352
00:13:55,535 --> 00:13:58,590
I have no authority to release these men.

353
00:13:58,610 --> 00:14:02,049
Stassen was unarmed, like the rest of his party.

354
00:14:02,069 --> 00:14:02,818
He answered:

355
00:14:02,838 --> 00:14:04,760
You have no authority, period.

356
00:14:06,150 --> 00:14:08,726
Inside the wire, the prisoners had already

357
00:14:08,746 --> 00:14:09,839
seen the boats.

358
00:14:09,859 --> 00:14:12,065
The Navy's photographs show them crowding

359
00:14:12,085 --> 00:14:14,661
the waterfront, waving the flags of the

360
00:14:14,681 --> 00:14:16,886
United States, Britain and the Netherlands.

361
00:14:16,906 --> 00:14:19,112
The Associated Press reporter wrote that

362
00:14:19,132 --> 00:14:20,966
they were delirious with joy,

363
00:14:20,986 --> 00:14:22,470
many of them weeping.

364
00:14:23,875 --> 00:14:26,114
Among them was Major Gregory Boyington,

365
00:14:26,134 --> 00:14:28,750
the Marine fighter ace known as Pappy.

366
00:14:28,770 --> 00:14:31,763
He told the reporters he had been beaten

367
00:14:31,783 --> 00:14:33,269
with a baseball bat.

368
00:14:33,289 --> 00:14:36,043
The senior prisoner at Omori was Commander

369
00:14:36,063 --> 00:14:38,800
Arthur Maher, the senior survivor of the

370
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:41,950
cruiser Houston, sunk in 1942.

371
00:14:42,650 --> 00:14:44,938
At ten past seven that evening,

372
00:14:44,958 --> 00:14:47,247
the first freed prisoners were carried

373
00:14:47,267 --> 00:14:48,401
aboard the Benevolence.

374
00:14:48,421 --> 00:14:51,094
The Navy gave them a new name:

375
00:14:51,114 --> 00:14:53,018
RAMPs, Rescued Allied Military Prisoners.

376
00:14:53,038 --> 00:14:54,942
By midnight, according to Halsey,

377
00:14:54,962 --> 00:14:57,635
seven hundred and ninety-four men had been

378
00:14:57,655 --> 00:14:58,405
brought out.

379
00:14:58,425 --> 00:15:01,098
The next morning, Commodore Simpson sent a

380
00:15:01,118 --> 00:15:04,176
radio message to the fleet about what his

381
00:15:04,196 --> 00:15:05,350
men had found.

382
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:09,532
The message survives in MacArthur's staff

383
00:15:09,552 --> 00:15:11,167
history, word for word.

384
00:15:11,187 --> 00:15:14,028
There has never been a blacker hellhole

385
00:15:14,048 --> 00:15:17,707
than the P O W hospital we are now

386
00:15:17,727 --> 00:15:20,568
evacuating, one half mile north of mooring.

387
00:15:20,588 --> 00:15:23,020
Approximately five hundred have now been

388
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:24,655
processed to Benevolence, including

389
00:15:24,675 --> 00:15:26,290
fractures, open wounds, concussions,

390
00:15:26,310 --> 00:15:28,743
burns, and in general the worst

391
00:15:28,763 --> 00:15:29,580
malnutrition imaginable.

392
00:15:30,950 --> 00:15:32,503
Simpson went on.

393
00:15:32,523 --> 00:15:34,601
Bestial beatings were common,

394
00:15:34,621 --> 00:15:38,780
especially at Ofuna, inquisitorial den of brutism.

395
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:41,995
MacArthur's staff later called Ofuna the

396
00:15:42,015 --> 00:15:43,457
Gestapo center of Japan.

397
00:15:43,477 --> 00:15:46,379
Then came the line his officers never forgot.

398
00:15:46,399 --> 00:15:50,398
The cheers of P O W as our boat hove into

399
00:15:50,418 --> 00:15:52,610
sight brought tears to our eyes.

400
00:15:53,850 --> 00:15:56,773
The Navy photographed some of the men on

401
00:15:56,793 --> 00:15:57,508
the Benevolence.

402
00:15:57,528 --> 00:15:59,715
One picture shows Private Alfred Sorenson

403
00:15:59,735 --> 00:16:03,238
of the U S Army, captured on Corregidor on

404
00:16:03,258 --> 00:16:06,670
the sixth of May, 1942, sitting in front

405
00:16:06,690 --> 00:16:09,123
of his first full meal in three years.

406
00:16:09,143 --> 00:16:11,577
He is looking at it, not eating yet.

407
00:16:11,597 --> 00:16:14,337
Another shows a man too weak to walk being

408
00:16:14,357 --> 00:16:16,790
carried down to a boat, past a crate

409
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:18,650
marked U S Navy Battle Ration.

410
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,140
Two days later the Japanese government

411
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:23,580
sent a formal protest.

412
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:26,820
A U S Naval landing unit of about one

413
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,980
hundred and fifty men, it said,

414
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,980
broke into the Shinagawa camp of the

415
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,013
Allied war prisoners and forcibly led away

416
00:16:35,033 --> 00:16:35,880
the prisoners.

417
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:38,047
The Navy filed the complaint.

418
00:16:38,067 --> 00:16:39,800
Nobody gave anyone back.

419
00:16:41,025 --> 00:16:43,856
If you want more prisoner of war stories

420
00:16:43,876 --> 00:16:45,994
told from the documents, subscribe to

421
00:16:46,014 --> 00:16:47,420
World War Two Frontlines.

422
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:50,653
And now, back to Atsugi, where the next

423
00:16:50,673 --> 00:16:52,399
afternoon the man Japan feared most was

424
00:16:52,419 --> 00:16:54,165
about to step out of a plane.

425
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:57,590
On the thirtieth of August,

426
00:16:57,610 --> 00:16:59,700
the transports started landing at Atsugi

427
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:01,380
one every three minutes.

428
00:17:01,400 --> 00:17:04,440
They brought the paratroopers of the 11th

429
00:17:04,460 --> 00:17:05,342
Airborne Division.

430
00:17:05,362 --> 00:17:08,048
By nightfall four thousand two hundred

431
00:17:08,068 --> 00:17:11,205
American soldiers had been flown into the

432
00:17:11,225 --> 00:17:12,558
heart of Japan.

433
00:17:12,578 --> 00:17:15,228
General Robert Eichelberger of the Eighth

434
00:17:15,248 --> 00:17:16,438
Army arrived that morning.

435
00:17:16,458 --> 00:17:18,858
This, he said, was the beachhead where he

436
00:17:18,878 --> 00:17:21,279
was supposed to land in the invasion of

437
00:17:21,299 --> 00:17:23,699
Japan, and he had never expected to get

438
00:17:23,719 --> 00:17:26,140
there by plane without a shot being fired.

439
00:17:27,450 --> 00:17:29,073
About two in the afternoon,

440
00:17:29,093 --> 00:17:31,590
a four-engine C-54 came in to land.

441
00:17:31,610 --> 00:17:34,630
Eichelberger wrote down its name:

442
00:17:34,650 --> 00:17:36,020
the Bataan.

443
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:38,897
MacArthur had ordered that there be no

444
00:17:38,917 --> 00:17:40,150
Japanese reception party.

445
00:17:41,425 --> 00:17:43,415
MacArthur came out with his corncob pipe

446
00:17:43,435 --> 00:17:45,425
in his mouth, and walked over to

447
00:17:45,445 --> 00:17:47,449
Eichelberger and the paratroopers.

448
00:17:47,469 --> 00:17:49,323
MacArthur's headquarters recorded what he

449
00:17:49,343 --> 00:17:50,073
told them:

450
00:17:50,093 --> 00:17:52,696
Melbourne to Tokyo was a long road,

451
00:17:52,716 --> 00:17:54,965
but this looks like the payoff.

452
00:17:56,300 --> 00:17:59,187
There is a famous story about this landing.

453
00:17:59,207 --> 00:18:01,730
MacArthur is said to have told his

454
00:18:01,750 --> 00:18:03,910
officers to take off their pistols,

455
00:18:03,930 --> 00:18:06,453
because if the Japanese meant to kill

456
00:18:06,473 --> 00:18:08,270
them, sidearms would be useless,

457
00:18:08,290 --> 00:18:11,177
and nothing would impress them more than a

458
00:18:11,197 --> 00:18:12,630
show of absolute fearlessness.

459
00:18:12,650 --> 00:18:14,810
The story appears in biographies written

460
00:18:14,830 --> 00:18:15,537
years later.

461
00:18:15,557 --> 00:18:20,260
It is not in the records of 1945.

462
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:22,978
What the records do show is that he chose

463
00:18:22,998 --> 00:18:25,696
to land in a country of millions of armed

464
00:18:25,716 --> 00:18:27,508
men with a few thousand paratroopers.

465
00:18:27,528 --> 00:18:29,340
Then he got into a car.

466
00:18:31,050 --> 00:18:33,443
The road from Atsugi to the New Grand

467
00:18:33,463 --> 00:18:36,514
Hotel in Yokohama ran for about fifteen miles.

468
00:18:36,534 --> 00:18:39,208
MacArthur's staff wrote that it was lined,

469
00:18:39,228 --> 00:18:41,902
the whole way, with thousands of armed

470
00:18:41,922 --> 00:18:43,442
Japanese soldiers and policemen.

471
00:18:43,462 --> 00:18:46,135
They stood at attention, but faced away

472
00:18:46,155 --> 00:18:47,310
from the road.

473
00:18:48,575 --> 00:18:50,331
It was a security measure,

474
00:18:50,351 --> 00:18:51,397
and an honor.

475
00:18:51,417 --> 00:18:53,884
In Japan, guards turned their backs that

476
00:18:53,904 --> 00:18:56,015
way only for the Imperial family.

477
00:18:56,035 --> 00:18:58,429
Eichelberger, riding in the convoy,

478
00:18:58,449 --> 00:19:01,274
wrote that until that day it had been done

479
00:19:01,294 --> 00:19:02,875
only for the Emperor himself.

480
00:19:04,225 --> 00:19:06,365
The convoy was not impressive.

481
00:19:06,385 --> 00:19:09,821
The Japanese had found whatever cars they could.

482
00:19:09,841 --> 00:19:12,413
The Navy's historian notes that MacArthur

483
00:19:12,433 --> 00:19:15,437
rode in an old American-made car that

484
00:19:15,457 --> 00:19:17,165
broke down several times.

485
00:19:17,185 --> 00:19:20,862
Eichelberger wrote that it took them two

486
00:19:20,882 --> 00:19:22,426
hours to go twenty miles.

487
00:19:22,446 --> 00:19:25,555
He also wrote that he did not draw an easy

488
00:19:25,575 --> 00:19:27,765
breath until MacArthur was inside the hotel.

489
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,160
That night MacArthur's party ate dinner at

490
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:33,480
the New Grand.

491
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,560
A photographer who was there wrote down

492
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:37,440
the menu.

493
00:19:37,460 --> 00:19:38,760
Hamburgers and grapes.

494
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:41,420
Five hundred paratroopers of the 11th

495
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:42,836
Airborne guarded the building.

496
00:19:42,856 --> 00:19:45,314
And the next evening, a thin general

497
00:19:45,334 --> 00:19:48,520
leaning on a cane walked into that dining room.

498
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:52,257
Lieutenant General Jonathan Wainwright had

499
00:19:52,277 --> 00:19:54,749
surrendered the American forces in the

500
00:19:54,769 --> 00:19:57,900
Philippines on Corregidor in May 1942,

501
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:00,787
after MacArthur had been ordered out to Australia.

502
00:20:00,807 --> 00:20:03,675
For more than three years he was a

503
00:20:03,695 --> 00:20:06,300
prisoner, in camps from Luzon to Formosa

504
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:07,119
to Manchuria.

505
00:20:07,139 --> 00:20:10,395
With him at the end was the British

506
00:20:10,415 --> 00:20:12,442
general who had surrendered Singapore,

507
00:20:12,462 --> 00:20:14,100
Lieutenant General Arthur Percival.

508
00:20:15,425 --> 00:20:17,117
Wainwright wrote that in the middle of the

509
00:20:17,137 --> 00:20:20,042
night at Mukden, an American officer named

510
00:20:20,062 --> 00:20:22,408
Major Lamar brought him a message.

511
00:20:22,428 --> 00:20:24,379
General MacArthur had invited General

512
00:20:24,399 --> 00:20:27,365
Percival and him to fly to Yokohama,

513
00:20:27,385 --> 00:20:30,350
to be with him at the formal surrender of

514
00:20:30,370 --> 00:20:31,365
the Japanese nation.

515
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:35,665
On the thirtieth of August he landed in

516
00:20:35,685 --> 00:20:38,277
Manila to be outfitted for the ceremony.

517
00:20:38,297 --> 00:20:40,516
An Associated Press reporter described him

518
00:20:40,536 --> 00:20:43,502
getting off the plane, pale and leaning on

519
00:20:43,522 --> 00:20:44,248
a cane.

520
00:20:44,268 --> 00:20:45,741
Wainwright said three words:

521
00:20:45,761 --> 00:20:46,880
This is wonderful.

522
00:20:48,275 --> 00:20:52,135
The next afternoon he landed at Atsugi.

523
00:20:52,155 --> 00:20:54,315
As he was driven to Yokohama,

524
00:20:54,335 --> 00:20:56,543
Japanese generals came to attention and

525
00:20:56,563 --> 00:20:57,285
saluted him.

526
00:20:57,305 --> 00:21:00,256
He wrote that this came after years of

527
00:21:00,276 --> 00:21:01,741
humiliating servility before Japanese

528
00:21:01,761 --> 00:21:04,340
privates, of being beaten for failing to

529
00:21:04,360 --> 00:21:05,825
bow to a sentry.

530
00:21:05,845 --> 00:21:08,796
That evening at the New Grand he met

531
00:21:08,816 --> 00:21:13,515
MacArthur for the first time since March 1942.

532
00:21:13,535 --> 00:21:16,116
Their reunion is a story of its own,

533
00:21:16,136 --> 00:21:18,718
and we told it in an earlier episode.

534
00:21:18,738 --> 00:21:20,669
That night, in Tokyo, the Japanese

535
00:21:20,689 --> 00:21:22,620
government still could not find anyone

536
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:23,615
willing to sign.

537
00:21:25,325 --> 00:21:27,856
Kase was blunt about it.

538
00:21:27,876 --> 00:21:30,781
Nobody, he wrote, wanted to volunteer for

539
00:21:30,801 --> 00:21:32,034
the odious duty.

540
00:21:32,054 --> 00:21:34,725
The prime minister, Prince Higashikuni,

541
00:21:34,745 --> 00:21:38,199
was the Emperor's uncle, and could not be sent.

542
00:21:38,219 --> 00:21:40,724
Prince Konoye, a former prime minister,

543
00:21:40,744 --> 00:21:41,058
refused.

544
00:21:41,078 --> 00:21:44,070
In the end the task fell to the foreign

545
00:21:44,090 --> 00:21:46,845
minister, Mamoru Shigemitsu.

546
00:21:46,865 --> 00:21:49,845
He accepted it as a sign of the Emperor's trust.

547
00:21:51,175 --> 00:21:54,223
Shigemitsu had lost a leg to a bomb in

548
00:21:54,243 --> 00:21:56,755
Shanghai in 1932.

549
00:21:56,775 --> 00:21:59,716
The army had to send someone too.

550
00:21:59,736 --> 00:22:03,099
It chose the Chief of the Army General

551
00:22:03,119 --> 00:22:04,955
Staff, General Yoshijiro Umezu,

552
00:22:04,975 --> 00:22:06,827
a man who had opposed surrender to the

553
00:22:06,847 --> 00:22:07,647
very end.

554
00:22:08,550 --> 00:22:11,256
Kase recorded what happened when Umezu was

555
00:22:11,276 --> 00:22:13,827
told, and he was careful to say it was

556
00:22:13,847 --> 00:22:14,970
only what people reported.

557
00:22:14,990 --> 00:22:17,698
Umezu went white with anger,

558
00:22:17,718 --> 00:22:20,462
it was said, and declared that if he was

559
00:22:20,482 --> 00:22:22,919
forced to do this, he would commit suicide.

560
00:22:22,939 --> 00:22:24,762
It took the Emperor's personal persuasion

561
00:22:24,782 --> 00:22:26,010
to make him go.

562
00:22:27,250 --> 00:22:28,680
Nine aides went with them,

563
00:22:28,700 --> 00:22:30,420
three each from the foreign ministry,

564
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:31,870
the army and the navy.

565
00:22:31,890 --> 00:22:34,826
Kase was one of them.

566
00:22:34,846 --> 00:22:37,534
The trip was kept secret, Kase wrote,

567
00:22:37,554 --> 00:22:40,290
in case extremists tried to stop them by force.

568
00:22:41,975 --> 00:22:44,543
They left Tokyo at about five in the

569
00:22:44,563 --> 00:22:46,484
morning on the second of September.

570
00:22:46,504 --> 00:22:49,589
The road to Yokohama ran for miles through

571
00:22:49,609 --> 00:22:52,204
the ruins left by the fire raids.

572
00:22:52,224 --> 00:22:54,819
On the cars, the little flags that

573
00:22:54,839 --> 00:22:57,434
normally flew on official cars had been

574
00:22:57,454 --> 00:22:58,181
taken off.

575
00:22:58,201 --> 00:23:01,057
In Yokohama the officers left their swords

576
00:23:01,077 --> 00:23:02,115
at the governor's office.

577
00:23:02,135 --> 00:23:06,351
Kase wrote one line about it that he

578
00:23:06,371 --> 00:23:07,499
clearly never forgot.

579
00:23:07,519 --> 00:23:09,410
Diplomats without flag, and soldiers

580
00:23:09,430 --> 00:23:10,230
without sword.

581
00:23:11,525 --> 00:23:14,844
At the quay four American destroyers were waiting.

582
00:23:14,864 --> 00:23:17,348
The Japanese were taken aboard the

583
00:23:17,368 --> 00:23:19,225
destroyer Lansdowne.

584
00:23:19,245 --> 00:23:21,845
The ride out to the Missouri took about an hour.

585
00:23:23,100 --> 00:23:25,948
On the battleship, the Navy had spent days

586
00:23:25,968 --> 00:23:26,307
preparing.

587
00:23:26,327 --> 00:23:28,100
Sailors had rehearsed the ceremony,

588
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:30,609
standing in for the guests and timing

589
00:23:30,629 --> 00:23:31,327
every step.

590
00:23:31,347 --> 00:23:34,553
And in a frame above the door of the

591
00:23:34,573 --> 00:23:37,063
captain's cabin hung a very old flag.

592
00:23:37,083 --> 00:23:39,932
It was the flag Commodore Perry had flown

593
00:23:39,952 --> 00:23:44,227
when his warships sailed into Tokyo Bay in 1853.

594
00:23:44,247 --> 00:23:46,466
A Navy courier had carried it nine

595
00:23:46,486 --> 00:23:47,745
thousand miles from Washington.

596
00:23:47,765 --> 00:23:49,663
It was too fragile to fly,

597
00:23:49,683 --> 00:23:51,902
so it had been backed with linen,

598
00:23:51,922 --> 00:23:54,800
and the stars ended up on the wrong side.

599
00:23:56,100 --> 00:23:59,460
On the deck that morning stood more men

600
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:02,418
who had come out of Japanese camps.

601
00:24:02,438 --> 00:24:05,375
The NBC radio announcer read their names

602
00:24:05,395 --> 00:24:06,642
on the air.

603
00:24:06,662 --> 00:24:09,178
Commander Arthur Maher of the Houston.

604
00:24:09,198 --> 00:24:10,868
A Navy torpedo pilot.

605
00:24:10,888 --> 00:24:14,800
A Marine officer captured on Corregidor.

606
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:17,697
A machinist's mate from the submarine Grenadier.

607
00:24:17,717 --> 00:24:20,593
And, just behind the place where MacArthur

608
00:24:20,613 --> 00:24:22,662
would stand, Wainwright and Percival.

609
00:24:22,682 --> 00:24:25,559
At eight fifty-six, the Japanese came up

610
00:24:25,579 --> 00:24:28,357
the ladder, and Kase began to count the

611
00:24:28,377 --> 00:24:29,260
little painted flags.

612
00:24:30,950 --> 00:24:33,060
At two minutes past nine, General

613
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:35,190
MacArthur walked briskly to the microphones.

614
00:24:35,210 --> 00:24:38,557
Kase was surprised by that alone.

615
00:24:38,577 --> 00:24:41,143
He wrote that it was their understanding

616
00:24:41,163 --> 00:24:42,990
there would be no speech.

617
00:24:43,010 --> 00:24:44,837
Halsey, standing nearby, noticed that

618
00:24:44,857 --> 00:24:47,054
MacArthur's voice was clear and firm,

619
00:24:47,074 --> 00:24:49,290
but that his hands were shaking.

620
00:24:50,650 --> 00:24:52,814
This is his voice, recorded on the

621
00:24:52,834 --> 00:24:53,770
Missouri that morning.

622
00:24:55,125 --> 00:24:59,995
We are gathered here, representatives of

623
00:25:00,015 --> 00:25:05,700
the major warring powers, to conclude a

624
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:11,425
solemn agreement whereby peace may be restored.

625
00:25:12,625 --> 00:25:16,983
The issues, involving divergent ideals and

626
00:25:17,003 --> 00:25:21,360
ideologies, have been determined on the

627
00:25:21,380 --> 00:25:24,279
battlefields of the world,

628
00:25:24,299 --> 00:25:30,865
and hence are not for our discussion or debate.

629
00:25:31,925 --> 00:25:34,131
Then he said something the Japanese had

630
00:25:34,151 --> 00:25:35,402
not expected to hear.

631
00:25:35,422 --> 00:25:37,946
Nor is it for us here, he said,

632
00:25:37,966 --> 00:25:40,171
to meet in a spirit of distrust,

633
00:25:40,191 --> 00:25:41,145
malice or hatred.

634
00:25:42,475 --> 00:25:45,002
It is my earnest hope, he went on,

635
00:25:45,022 --> 00:25:47,231
and indeed the hope of all mankind,

636
00:25:47,251 --> 00:25:49,460
that from this solemn occasion a better

637
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:52,007
world shall emerge out of the blood and

638
00:25:52,027 --> 00:25:53,280
carnage of the past.

639
00:25:53,300 --> 00:25:55,190
A world founded upon faith and

640
00:25:55,210 --> 00:25:57,101
understanding, a world dedicated to the

641
00:25:57,121 --> 00:25:59,648
dignity of man and the fulfillment of his

642
00:25:59,668 --> 00:26:01,240
most cherished wish, for freedom,

643
00:26:01,260 --> 00:26:02,215
tolerance and justice.

644
00:26:03,550 --> 00:26:05,904
Kase listened, and he understood exactly

645
00:26:05,924 --> 00:26:07,371
what was being offered.

646
00:26:07,391 --> 00:26:09,205
The man at the microphone,

647
00:26:09,225 --> 00:26:12,139
he wrote, could exact his pound of flesh

648
00:26:12,159 --> 00:26:13,606
if he so chose.

649
00:26:13,626 --> 00:26:16,174
He could impose a humiliating penalty if

650
00:26:16,194 --> 00:26:17,274
he so desired.

651
00:26:17,294 --> 00:26:19,475
And yet he pleaded for freedom,

652
00:26:19,495 --> 00:26:20,575
tolerance and justice.

653
00:26:20,595 --> 00:26:23,510
For a man who had come aboard expecting

654
00:26:23,530 --> 00:26:25,663
the worst humiliation, Kase wrote,

655
00:26:25,683 --> 00:26:27,350
this was a complete surprise.

656
00:26:28,725 --> 00:26:30,985
Then MacArthur turned to the Japanese.

657
00:26:32,475 --> 00:26:36,790
I now invite the representatives of the

658
00:26:36,810 --> 00:26:40,506
Emperor of Japan and the Japanese

659
00:26:40,526 --> 00:26:43,603
Government and the Japanese Imperial

660
00:26:43,623 --> 00:26:46,700
General Headquarters to sign the

661
00:26:46,720 --> 00:26:51,055
instrument of surrender at the places indicated.

662
00:26:51,975 --> 00:26:53,695
The crowded deck went silent.

663
00:26:55,400 --> 00:26:58,103
Shigemitsu walked to the table first.

664
00:26:58,123 --> 00:27:01,025
He took off his silk top hat and his

665
00:27:01,045 --> 00:27:03,622
gloves, and he had trouble with the chair

666
00:27:03,642 --> 00:27:04,920
because of his leg.

667
00:27:04,940 --> 00:27:07,517
He seemed not to know where to sign.

668
00:27:07,537 --> 00:27:09,789
MacArthur turned and told his chief of

669
00:27:09,809 --> 00:27:11,088
staff, General Richard Sutherland,

670
00:27:11,108 --> 00:27:13,360
to go and show him the line.

671
00:27:13,380 --> 00:27:15,665
Shigemitsu signed.

672
00:27:15,685 --> 00:27:18,580
Then he paused and looked up at Kase,

673
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:20,148
who checked his wristwatch.

674
00:27:20,168 --> 00:27:22,520
It was four minutes past nine.

675
00:27:23,825 --> 00:27:25,857
General Umezu came next.

676
00:27:25,877 --> 00:27:28,418
He did not sit down at all.

677
00:27:28,438 --> 00:27:30,613
Halsey wrote that he signed briskly,

678
00:27:30,633 --> 00:27:31,345
standing up.

679
00:27:31,365 --> 00:27:34,271
The radio announcer said he was in very

680
00:27:34,291 --> 00:27:35,735
much of a rush.

681
00:27:35,755 --> 00:27:37,930
It was six minutes past nine,

682
00:27:37,950 --> 00:27:40,145
and the Japanese part was done.

683
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:43,946
Then MacArthur stepped to the table himself.

684
00:27:43,966 --> 00:27:46,879
And at this moment he said something that

685
00:27:46,899 --> 00:27:50,178
is not in the typed text of his speech.

686
00:27:50,198 --> 00:27:53,110
The radio recording of the ceremony caught it.

687
00:27:53,130 --> 00:27:55,310
MacArthur said the Supreme Commander would

688
00:27:55,330 --> 00:27:58,609
now sign on behalf of all the nations at

689
00:27:58,629 --> 00:27:59,708
war with Japan.

690
00:27:59,728 --> 00:28:00,808
Then he said:

691
00:28:00,828 --> 00:28:02,641
Will General Wainwright and General

692
00:28:02,661 --> 00:28:04,840
Percival step forward and accompany me

693
00:28:04,860 --> 00:28:05,960
while I sign?

694
00:28:07,275 --> 00:28:10,575
In 1942 these two men had surrendered to

695
00:28:10,595 --> 00:28:14,068
Japan, one at Corregidor and one at Singapore.

696
00:28:14,088 --> 00:28:16,787
Now they stood at MacArthur's shoulders

697
00:28:16,807 --> 00:28:19,053
while he signed Japan's surrender.

698
00:28:19,073 --> 00:28:22,225
The announcer said MacArthur's hand was shaking.

699
00:28:22,245 --> 00:28:24,038
He used several pens.

700
00:28:24,058 --> 00:28:26,757
Halfway through his signature he turned

701
00:28:26,777 --> 00:28:29,929
and handed the first pen to Wainwright.

702
00:28:29,949 --> 00:28:32,215
The second went to Percival.

703
00:28:33,525 --> 00:28:36,447
The rest of the signing took fourteen minutes.

704
00:28:36,467 --> 00:28:38,654
Nimitz signed for the United States,

705
00:28:38,674 --> 00:28:40,860
then China, Britain, the Soviet Union,

706
00:28:40,880 --> 00:28:41,963
Australia, Canada, France,

707
00:28:41,983 --> 00:28:43,802
the Netherlands and New Zealand.

708
00:28:43,822 --> 00:28:45,641
The Canadian, Colonel Lawrence Moore

709
00:28:45,661 --> 00:28:48,583
Cosgrave, who was blind in one eye from

710
00:28:48,603 --> 00:28:51,157
the First World War, signed the Japanese

711
00:28:51,177 --> 00:28:54,099
copy on the wrong line, and pushed every

712
00:28:54,119 --> 00:28:56,306
signature after him down one place.

713
00:28:56,326 --> 00:28:59,248
The New Zealander, whose name ended up in

714
00:28:59,268 --> 00:29:01,454
the margin, called himself a humble

715
00:29:01,474 --> 00:29:02,945
footnote to the document.

716
00:29:04,650 --> 00:29:06,480
At twenty-two minutes past nine,

717
00:29:06,500 --> 00:29:09,070
the last signature was on the page.

718
00:29:09,090 --> 00:29:11,310
MacArthur went back to the microphones.

719
00:29:12,700 --> 00:29:19,422
Let us pray that peace be now restored to

720
00:29:19,442 --> 00:29:26,164
the world, and that God will preserve it always.

721
00:29:26,184 --> 00:29:29,180
These proceedings are closed.

722
00:29:30,300 --> 00:29:32,519
Kase wrote that MacArthur faced the

723
00:29:32,539 --> 00:29:34,509
Japanese delegation when he said those

724
00:29:34,529 --> 00:29:36,520
last words, and said them curtly.

725
00:29:37,725 --> 00:29:41,210
As he said it, the clouds over the bay broke.

726
00:29:41,230 --> 00:29:44,014
The NBC announcer described it on the air,

727
00:29:44,034 --> 00:29:46,482
and Kase described it in his book,

728
00:29:46,502 --> 00:29:49,110
the sun coming through over Tokyo Bay.

729
00:29:49,130 --> 00:29:50,612
Then came the sound.

730
00:29:50,632 --> 00:29:53,240
The Navy's figure is four hundred and

731
00:29:53,260 --> 00:29:55,869
fifty carrier planes from the Third Fleet,

732
00:29:55,889 --> 00:29:58,121
flying over the Missouri in massed

733
00:29:58,141 --> 00:29:59,248
formation, mast high.

734
00:29:59,268 --> 00:30:02,505
A few minutes later the B-29s came over.

735
00:30:03,750 --> 00:30:05,650
Before they left, the diplomats checked

736
00:30:05,670 --> 00:30:08,210
their copy of the surrender and found the

737
00:30:08,230 --> 00:30:08,850
Canadian's mistake.

738
00:30:08,870 --> 00:30:12,510
Kase and his colleague Katsuo Okazaki

739
00:30:12,530 --> 00:30:14,710
would not accept a document with

740
00:30:14,730 --> 00:30:16,543
signatures on the wrong lines.

741
00:30:16,563 --> 00:30:19,110
General Sutherland sat down at the table,

742
00:30:19,130 --> 00:30:20,943
crossed out the country names,

743
00:30:20,963 --> 00:30:23,510
wrote in the right ones by hand,

744
00:30:23,530 --> 00:30:24,977
and initialled every change.

745
00:30:24,997 --> 00:30:27,930
Only then did the Japanese accept their copy.

746
00:30:29,225 --> 00:30:31,068
At nine twenty-nine, after thirty-three

747
00:30:31,088 --> 00:30:34,048
minutes on board, they were piped over the

748
00:30:34,068 --> 00:30:37,028
side, with the honors due to men whose

749
00:30:37,048 --> 00:30:39,636
country was now officially at peace with

750
00:30:39,656 --> 00:30:40,754
the United States.

751
00:30:40,774 --> 00:30:44,479
No one saluted them and no one shook their hands.

752
00:30:44,499 --> 00:30:46,342
But nobody insulted them either.

753
00:30:46,362 --> 00:30:48,577
After the cease-fire, Nimitz had ordered

754
00:30:48,597 --> 00:30:51,185
that the use of insulting epithets about

755
00:30:51,205 --> 00:30:53,792
the Japanese did not now become officers

756
00:30:53,812 --> 00:30:55,675
of the United States Navy.

757
00:30:56,375 --> 00:30:58,453
On the way back, in the destroyer's gun

758
00:30:58,473 --> 00:31:01,381
room, Kase heard MacArthur on the radio,

759
00:31:01,401 --> 00:31:02,657
speaking to America.

760
00:31:02,677 --> 00:31:04,783
Today the guns are silent,

761
00:31:04,803 --> 00:31:05,634
MacArthur said.

762
00:31:05,654 --> 00:31:07,760
A great tragedy has ended.

763
00:31:07,780 --> 00:31:10,312
A great victory has been won.

764
00:31:10,332 --> 00:31:13,714
He spoke of the long, tortuous trail from

765
00:31:13,734 --> 00:31:17,715
those grim days of Bataan and Corregidor.

766
00:31:17,735 --> 00:31:20,375
And in that gun room, Kase began to write.

767
00:31:22,075 --> 00:31:25,326
What he was writing was a report for the Emperor.

768
00:31:25,346 --> 00:31:27,615
As soon as the delegation reached Tokyo,

769
00:31:27,635 --> 00:31:30,249
Shigemitsu took it straight to the palace,

770
00:31:30,269 --> 00:31:32,355
where, Kase wrote, the Emperor was

771
00:31:32,375 --> 00:31:33,015
anxiously waiting.

772
00:31:33,035 --> 00:31:36,956
Kase never published the report word for word.

773
00:31:36,976 --> 00:31:40,035
But in his book he told what was in it.

774
00:31:41,225 --> 00:31:42,905
He had described the speech,

775
00:31:42,925 --> 00:31:45,625
the deck, and the man at the microphone.

776
00:31:45,645 --> 00:31:48,005
And then he had asked a question.

777
00:31:48,025 --> 00:31:50,385
Would it have been possible for us,

778
00:31:50,405 --> 00:31:52,425
he asked, had we been victorious,

779
00:31:52,445 --> 00:31:54,805
to embrace the vanquished with a similar

780
00:31:54,825 --> 00:31:55,145
magnanimity?

781
00:31:55,165 --> 00:31:58,205
He answered it himself in the next line of

782
00:31:58,225 --> 00:31:58,885
his book.

783
00:31:58,905 --> 00:32:00,945
Clearly, it would have been different.

784
00:32:02,250 --> 00:32:04,784
When Shigemitsu came back from the palace,

785
00:32:04,804 --> 00:32:07,069
he told Kase what had happened.

786
00:32:07,089 --> 00:32:09,147
The Emperor had read the report,

787
00:32:09,167 --> 00:32:11,225
and had nodded, with a sigh,

788
00:32:11,245 --> 00:32:11,918
in agreement.

789
00:32:11,938 --> 00:32:14,350
We know this only from Kase,

790
00:32:14,370 --> 00:32:17,110
who heard it from Shigemitsu.

791
00:32:17,130 --> 00:32:18,730
No one else was in the room.

792
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:23,386
Kase ended that chapter with one more line.

793
00:32:23,406 --> 00:32:25,276
We were defeated, he wrote,

794
00:32:25,296 --> 00:32:26,788
by a nobler ideal.

795
00:32:26,808 --> 00:32:30,240
He was writing in 1950, under the

796
00:32:30,260 --> 00:32:32,640
occupation, about a man he admired.

797
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:35,440
MacArthur's own staff wrote much the same:

798
00:32:35,460 --> 00:32:38,640
that his policies took Japan by complete surprise.

799
00:32:38,660 --> 00:32:40,640
Neither of them was neutral.

800
00:32:40,660 --> 00:32:43,440
But the fears listed in the Tokyo

801
00:32:43,460 --> 00:32:46,260
newspapers in August did not come true.

802
00:32:47,925 --> 00:32:51,327
General Umezu was tried by the Tokyo war

803
00:32:51,347 --> 00:32:54,236
crimes tribunal and sentenced to life in prison.

804
00:32:54,256 --> 00:32:58,595
He died of cancer in prison in January 1949.

805
00:32:58,615 --> 00:33:02,058
Shigemitsu was sentenced to seven years,

806
00:33:02,078 --> 00:33:04,474
the lightest sentence the tribunal gave.

807
00:33:04,494 --> 00:33:07,933
He was paroled in 1950 and became Japan's

808
00:33:07,953 --> 00:33:10,705
foreign minister again a few years later.

809
00:33:10,725 --> 00:33:14,597
Toshikazu Kase became Japan's first

810
00:33:14,617 --> 00:33:16,583
ambassador to the United Nations.

811
00:33:16,603 --> 00:33:20,575
He lived to the age of one hundred and one.

812
00:33:21,275 --> 00:33:22,557
Wainwright was flown home,

813
00:33:22,577 --> 00:33:24,834
and on the tenth of September President

814
00:33:24,854 --> 00:33:27,763
Truman gave him the Medal of Honor in the

815
00:33:27,783 --> 00:33:28,739
White House garden.

816
00:33:28,759 --> 00:33:32,846
He died in 1953, eight years to the day

817
